yteConnectionConfig.connect_timeout_seconds and pool_startup_timeout_seconds, and AsyncpgDriver now reads both (asyncpg_driver.py:215-234, 870-871). It adds no unit test. connect_timeout_seconds is pinned indirectly: the leak test's elapsed < connect_timeout+5 (6.5s) would fail at the 10s default. pool_startup_timeout_seconds is not observed anywhere. In test_four_slow_serialized_logins the budget is max(3.5, 1.5*5) = 7.5s, so the floor never binds, and a driver that ignored the field (or went back to the original bug, a fixed DEFAULT_POOL_STARTUP_TIMEOUT_SECONDS budget = 30s) passes: four 1s logins fit in 30s. The old full-scale test failed the fixed-30s budget (36s > 30s). The gt=0 validation is untested too, and the defaults pin lives only in an integration test. Fix: a unit test through the driver's front door. Replace the module's public create_pool_with_startup_timeout with a recorder, then assert the startup_timeout/connect_timeout it is given: once where the floor binds (e.g. floor 100, pool_min_size 1), once where connect*(n+1) binds. Add a validation case for gt=0. Whether the fields should exist at all is the owner's #1154; this finding is only about coverage.", "files": ["packages/datasources/src/threetears/datasources/config.py", "packages/datasources/src/threetears/datasources/drivers/asyncpg_driver.py", "packages/datasources/tests/integration/test_asyncpg_driver_pool_start_live.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "No test evidence for the reviewed tree (prawduct-hook test-status: stale, no .test-evidence.json)", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The builders report 3x green pool runs per converted file on their own branches. No run covers the merged tree 40d781304, and the pool is paused for a testament update. Record the full unit and integration pool counts for this tree when the separate run lands. Give every integration skip with its reason, per CLAUDE.md.", "files": ["scripts/test-integration.sh"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "Renewal slack halved in absolute terms in timer tests that run on a loaded pool (unverified)", "recommendation": "Scope: class, bounded to the span's timer tests that shrank a TTL. Each kept its ratio, but the absolute margin a late renewal or login can use shrank. test_held_lease_live: TTL 2s/renew 0.5s became 1s/0.25s, so a renewal may now run 0.75s late (was 1.5s). test_a_bucket_an_old_release_created: TTL 2s/heartbeat 0.4s became 1s/0.2s, slack 0.8s (was 1.6s). test_four_slow_serialized_logins: each proxied login now has 0.5s above its 1.0s hold (was 1.0s). On a 30-slot host, an event-loop stall of that size reads as a lease or pool regression, not as load. The other conversions in the span either keep the old bound or have a generous failure-only one. Builders reported 3x green; this review could not run them (pool paused). Fix, if the full pool run shows any flake: keep the TTL at 1s but renew at a smaller fraction (renew_every=0.1s gives 0.9s), or accept the trade with a stated reason.", "files": ["packages/core/tests/integration/test_held_lease_live.py", "packages/core/tests/integration/test_distributed_lock_round_trip.py", "packages/datasources/tests/integration/test_asyncpg_driver_pool_start_live.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "severity": "note", "title": "claude_cli_pool._alive treats a zombie as not running: callers checked, no regression found; still_ours' zombie case is pinned only through kill_process_tree", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Checked every caller of _alive. kill_process_tree's grace loop and SIGKILL pass: SIGKILL to a zombie was already a no-op. still_ours, and through it _kill_if_still_ours: a zombie CLI used to get SIGTERM, a full grace, then SIGKILL, all no-ops, so skipping them changes only time. Its children were reparented at exit, so the old path never reached them either. The stopping-pid list at claude_cli_pool.py:1391 and the orphan sweep (via kill_process_tree) are fine. Without /proc, _stat_tokens returns None and the answer is signal 0's, as before. The new test pins the kill path. Nothing pins still_ours() == False for an unreaped child: test_a_recycled_pid... waits on the CLI first. A one-line assertion before cli.wait() would pin it. Separately, eb1ea4fde's message says an abandoned session's CLI is one 'which nothing awaits'. Whether such a child is ever reaped (asyncio's child watcher, or nothing once its loop is closed) is not examined here. A zombie per abandoned session would be a slow process-table leak; check it, or confirm the watcher reaps it.", "files": ["packages/models/src/threetears/models/claude_cli_pool.py", "packages/models/tests/unit/models/test_claude_cli_pool.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "severity": "note", "title": "Monkeypatching threetears.nats.client.REAUTH_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS: low correctness risk, with a silent failure mode", "recommendation": "Scope: class (any test patching a public module constant; precedent test_connection_handover.py). The patched value, 5s, sits above the server's 2s callout timeout. So each expected-to-fail renewal still meets the server's refusal before its own bound, as with the shipped 10s, and the docstring says so. In the superseded test the patch is scoped to the one call that must raise. The risk is silent decay, not masking. The name is a Final re-bound in client's namespace, so mypy cannot see the patch. If client.py ever reads it through credential_renewal, or as a default argument, the patch stops applying: the tests still pass, just 10s per renewal slower. A connect or renewal parameter would be the checked front door; that is #1154's question.", "files": ["packages/nats/tests/integration/test_credential_refusal_live.py"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "note", "title": "Record-lint relay: five suite-total-claim NOTEs, all outside the waits span", "recommendatio